          {
            "heading": "Part E: Solved examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Solved examples",
            "body": "(Jahan zaroorat ho, **1 cm² = 0.0001 m²** aur **1 cm = 0.01 m** lo. Ratio nikalte waqt dono area ek hi unit mein hone chahiye.)\n\n**Example 2 (output force nikalna):** Chhote piston ka area 5 cm², bade piston ka area 500 cm². Chhote piston par 50 N force lagaya. Bade piston par force?\n\n- A ÷ a = 500 cm² ÷ 5 cm² = 100\n- F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 50 N × 100 = **5000 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ ÷ a = 50 N ÷ 5 cm² = 10 N/cm². F₂ ÷ A = 5000 N ÷ 500 cm² = 10 N/cm² ✅ (pressure barabar)\n\n**Example 3 (input force nikalna):** Ek lift ko 8000 N ka bojh uthana hai. Chhote piston ka area 20 cm² aur bade piston ka area 800 cm² hai. Chhote piston par kitna force lagana padega?\n\n- A ÷ a = 800 cm² ÷ 20 cm² = 40\n- F₁ = F₂ ÷ (A ÷ a) = 8000 N ÷ 40 = **200 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 200 N × 40 = 8000 N ✅\n\n**Example 4 (piston ka area nikalna):** Chhote piston par 200 N force lagane se bade piston par 20000 N force chahiye. Chhote piston ka area 4 cm² hai. Bade piston ka area kitna hona chahiye?\n\n- Force ka ratio = F₂ ÷ F₁ = 20000 N ÷ 200 N = 100\n- A = a × (F₂ ÷ F₁) = 4 cm² × 100 = **400 cm²**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 200 N × (400 cm² ÷ 4 cm²) = 200 N × 100 = 20000 N ✅\n\n**Example 5 (radius se area ka ratio):** Ek hydraulic press ke chhote piston ki radius 1 cm aur bade piston ki radius 10 cm hai. Chhote piston par 30 N force lagaya. Bade piston par force?\n\nArea A = π r². Dono ke liye π barabar hai, isliye ratio mein cancel ho jata hai:\n\n- A ÷ a = π (10 cm)² ÷ π (1 cm)² = (10)² ÷ (1)² = 100 ÷ 1 = **100**\n\n(Dhyan: radius 10 guna hai, par **area 10 guna nahi, 100 guna** hai, kyunki area radius ka square hota hai.)\n\n- F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 30 N × 100 = **3000 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** π lekar dekhein (π = 3.14): a = 3.14 × 1² = 3.14 cm², A = 3.14 × 10² = 314 cm². A ÷ a = 314 ÷ 3.14 = 100 ✅\n\n**Example 6 (dono pistons ka distance):** Chhote piston ka area 10 cm², bade piston ka area 500 cm² hai. Chhote piston ko 25 cm neeche dabaya gaya. Bada piston kitna upar uthega?\n\nDono taraf liquid ki matra barabar: a × d₁ = A × d₂\n\n- 10 cm² × 25 cm = 250 cm³ liquid chhote cylinder se gaya\n- d₂ = (a × d₁) ÷ A = 250 cm³ ÷ 500 cm² = **0.5 cm**\n\n**Jaanch:** A × d₂ = 500 cm² × 0.5 cm = 250 cm³ = a × d₁ ✅\n\n**Example 7 (piston ke neeche pressure):** Ek chhote piston ka area 20 cm² hai aur uspar 200 N force lagaya gaya. Tel mein pressure kitna bana?\n\nPehle area ko m² mein badlo:\n\n- a = 20 cm² = 20 × 0.0001 m² = 0.002 m²\n\nAb:\n\n- P = F ÷ a = 200 N ÷ 0.002 m² = **100000 Pa** (= 1 × 10⁵ Pa)\n\n**Jaanch:** P × a = 100000 Pa × 0.002 m² = 200 N ✅\n\n(Agar cm² mein hi chhod dete to 200 N ÷ 20 cm² = 10 N/cm² milta, jo sahi hai par Pa nahi hai. Pa ke liye m² zaroori hai.)\n\n**Example 8 (hydraulic brake):** Ek car ke brake mein pedal se master piston par 60 N ka force pahunchta hai. Master piston ka area 2 cm² hai. Pahiye ke piston ka area 8 cm² hai. (a) Tel mein pressure? (b) Pahiye ke piston par force?\n\n- Master piston ka area: a = 2 cm² = 2 × 0.0001 m² = 0.0002 m²\n- Pahiye ke piston ka area: A = 8 cm² = 0.0008 m²\n\n(a) P = F ÷ a = 60 N ÷ 0.0002 m² = **300000 Pa** (= 3 × 10⁵ Pa)\n\n(b) Pascal's law se pahiye ke piston par bhi wahi pressure hai:\n\n- F = P × A = 300000 Pa × 0.0008 m² = **240 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** Area ratio = 8 ÷ 2 = 4, aur force = 60 N × 4 = 240 N ✅\n\n**Example 9 (dono pistons ke pressure ki tulna):** Chhote piston (area 2 cm²) par 20 N aur bade piston (area 200 cm²) par 2000 N force hai. Dono jagah pressure kitna hai? Kya ye barabar hain?\n\n- Chhota piston: a = 2 cm² = 0.0002 m². P₁ = 20 N ÷ 0.0002 m² = **100000 Pa**\n- Bada piston: A = 200 cm² = 0.02 m². P₂ = 2000 N ÷ 0.02 m² = **100000 Pa**\n\nP₁ = P₂ ✅. **Pressure barabar hai, force alag.** Bade piston par force 2000 ÷ 20 = 100 guna hai kyunki area bhi 200 ÷ 2 = 100 guna hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** 100000 Pa × 0.0002 m² = 20 N ✅ aur 100000 Pa × 0.02 m² = 2000 N ✅\n\n**Example 10 (work ki jaanch):** Ek hydraulic machine ka a = 5 cm², A = 125 cm² hai. Chhote piston par 40 N force lagaya aur use 50 cm neeche dabaya. Bade piston par force aur distance? Dono taraf F × d barabar hai kya?\n\n- A ÷ a = 125 cm² ÷ 5 cm² = 25\n- F₂ = 40 N × 25 = **1000 N**\n- d₂ = d₁ ÷ 25 = 50 cm ÷ 25 = **2 cm**\n\nAb F × d, distance ko metre mein badal kar (50 cm = 0.5 m, 2 cm = 0.02 m):\n\n- Chhote piston: F₁ × d₁ = 40 N × 0.5 m = **20 N·m**\n- Bade piston: F₂ × d₂ = 1000 N × 0.02 m = **20 N·m** ✅\n\nDono barabar: machine ne force 25 guna kiya, par distance 25 guna ghata diya. Kuch free nahi mila.\n\n**Example 11 (radii se input force):** Ek lift mein chhote piston ki radius 2 cm aur bade piston ki radius 14 cm hai. 4900 N ka bojh uthana hai. Chhote piston par kitna force chahiye?\n\n- A ÷ a = (14 cm)² ÷ (2 cm)² = 196 cm² ÷ 4 cm² = **49**\n- F₁ = F₂ ÷ (A ÷ a) = 4900 N ÷ 49 = **100 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ × 49 = 100 N × 49 = 4900 N ✅ (Agar galti se radius ka ratio 7 le lete to F₁ = 700 N aata, jo galat hai.)",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Jahan zaroorat ho, **1 cm² = 0.0001 m²** aur **1 cm = 0.01 m** lo. Ratio nikalte waqt dono area ek hi unit mein hone chahiye.)\n\n**Example 2 (output force nikalna):** Chhote piston ka area 5 cm², bade piston ka area 500 cm². Chhote piston par 50 N force lagaya. Bade piston par force?\n\n- A ÷ a = 500 cm² ÷ 5 cm² = 100\n- F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 50 N × 100 = **5000 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ ÷ a = 50 N ÷ 5 cm² = 10 N/cm². F₂ ÷ A = 5000 N ÷ 500 cm² = 10 N/cm² ✅ (pressure barabar)\n\n**Example 3 (input force nikalna):** Ek lift ko 8000 N ka bojh uthana hai. Chhote piston ka area 20 cm² aur bade piston ka area 800 cm² hai. Chhote piston par kitna force lagana padega?\n\n- A ÷ a = 800 cm² ÷ 20 cm² = 40\n- F₁ = F₂ ÷ (A ÷ a) = 8000 N ÷ 40 = **200 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 200 N × 40 = 8000 N ✅\n\n**Example 4 (piston ka area nikalna):** Chhote piston par 200 N force lagane se bade piston par 20000 N force chahiye. Chhote piston ka area 4 cm² hai. Bade piston ka area kitna hona chahiye?\n\n- Force ka ratio = F₂ ÷ F₁ = 20000 N ÷ 200 N = 100\n- A = a × (F₂ ÷ F₁) = 4 cm² × 100 = **400 cm²**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 200 N × (400 cm² ÷ 4 cm²) = 200 N × 100 = 20000 N ✅\n\n**Example 5 (radius se area ka ratio):** Ek hydraulic press ke chhote piston ki radius 1 cm aur bade piston ki radius 10 cm hai. Chhote piston par 30 N force lagaya. Bade piston par force?\n\nArea A = π r². Dono ke liye π barabar hai, isliye ratio mein cancel ho jata hai:\n\n- A ÷ a = π (10 cm)² ÷ π (1 cm)² = (10)² ÷ (1)² = 100 ÷ 1 = **100**\n\n(Dhyan: radius 10 guna hai, par **area 10 guna nahi, 100 guna** hai, kyunki area radius ka square hota hai.)\n\n- F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a) = 30 N × 100 = **3000 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** π lekar dekhein (π = 3.14): a = 3.14 × 1² = 3.14 cm², A = 3.14 × 10² = 314 cm². A ÷ a = 314 ÷ 3.14 = 100 ✅\n\n**Example 6 (dono pistons ka distance):** Chhote piston ka area 10 cm², bade piston ka area 500 cm² hai. Chhote piston ko 25 cm neeche dabaya gaya. Bada piston kitna upar uthega?\n\nDono taraf liquid ki matra barabar: a × d₁ = A × d₂\n\n- 10 cm² × 25 cm = 250 cm³ liquid chhote cylinder se gaya\n- d₂ = (a × d₁) ÷ A = 250 cm³ ÷ 500 cm² = **0.5 cm**\n\n**Jaanch:** A × d₂ = 500 cm² × 0.5 cm = 250 cm³ = a × d₁ ✅\n\n**Example 7 (piston ke neeche pressure):** Ek chhote piston ka area 20 cm² hai aur uspar 200 N force lagaya gaya. Tel mein pressure kitna bana?\n\nPehle area ko m² mein badlo:\n\n- a = 20 cm² = 20 × 0.0001 m² = 0.002 m²\n\nAb:\n\n- P = F ÷ a = 200 N ÷ 0.002 m² = **100000 Pa** (= 1 × 10⁵ Pa)\n\n**Jaanch:** P × a = 100000 Pa × 0.002 m² = 200 N ✅\n\n(Agar cm² mein hi chhod dete to 200 N ÷ 20 cm² = 10 N/cm² milta, jo sahi hai par Pa nahi hai. Pa ke liye m² zaroori hai.)\n\n**Example 8 (hydraulic brake):** Ek car ke brake mein pedal se master piston par 60 N ka force pahunchta hai. Master piston ka area 2 cm² hai. Pahiye ke piston ka area 8 cm² hai. (a) Tel mein pressure? (b) Pahiye ke piston par force?\n\n- Master piston ka area: a = 2 cm² = 2 × 0.0001 m² = 0.0002 m²\n- Pahiye ke piston ka area: A = 8 cm² = 0.0008 m²\n\n(a) P = F ÷ a = 60 N ÷ 0.0002 m² = **300000 Pa** (= 3 × 10⁵ Pa)\n\n(b) Pascal's law se pahiye ke piston par bhi wahi pressure hai:\n\n- F = P × A = 300000 Pa × 0.0008 m² = **240 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** Area ratio = 8 ÷ 2 = 4, aur force = 60 N × 4 = 240 N ✅\n\n**Example 9 (dono pistons ke pressure ki tulna):** Chhote piston (area 2 cm²) par 20 N aur bade piston (area 200 cm²) par 2000 N force hai. Dono jagah pressure kitna hai? Kya ye barabar hain?\n\n- Chhota piston: a = 2 cm² = 0.0002 m². P₁ = 20 N ÷ 0.0002 m² = **100000 Pa**\n- Bada piston: A = 200 cm² = 0.02 m². P₂ = 2000 N ÷ 0.02 m² = **100000 Pa**\n\nP₁ = P₂ ✅. **Pressure barabar hai, force alag.** Bade piston par force 2000 ÷ 20 = 100 guna hai kyunki area bhi 200 ÷ 2 = 100 guna hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** 100000 Pa × 0.0002 m² = 20 N ✅ aur 100000 Pa × 0.02 m² = 2000 N ✅\n\n**Example 10 (work ki jaanch):** Ek hydraulic machine ka a = 5 cm², A = 125 cm² hai. Chhote piston par 40 N force lagaya aur use 50 cm neeche dabaya. Bade piston par force aur distance? Dono taraf F × d barabar hai kya?\n\n- A ÷ a = 125 cm² ÷ 5 cm² = 25\n- F₂ = 40 N × 25 = **1000 N**\n- d₂ = d₁ ÷ 25 = 50 cm ÷ 25 = **2 cm**\n\nAb F × d, distance ko metre mein badal kar (50 cm = 0.5 m, 2 cm = 0.02 m):\n\n- Chhote piston: F₁ × d₁ = 40 N × 0.5 m = **20 N·m**\n- Bade piston: F₂ × d₂ = 1000 N × 0.02 m = **20 N·m** ✅\n\nDono barabar: machine ne force 25 guna kiya, par distance 25 guna ghata diya. Kuch free nahi mila.\n\n**Example 11 (radii se input force):** Ek lift mein chhote piston ki radius 2 cm aur bade piston ki radius 14 cm hai. 4900 N ka bojh uthana hai. Chhote piston par kitna force chahiye?\n\n- A ÷ a = (14 cm)² ÷ (2 cm)² = 196 cm² ÷ 4 cm² = **49**\n- F₁ = F₂ ÷ (A ÷ a) = 4900 N ÷ 49 = **100 N**\n\n**Jaanch:** F₁ × 49 = 100 N × 49 = 4900 N ✅ (Agar galti se radius ka ratio 7 le lete to F₁ = 700 N aata, jo galat hai.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"Hydraulic lift se extra energy ya work milta hai.\"**\n\nNahi. Force 100 guna hota hai to distance 100 guna ghat jata hai. Example 1 mein F₁ d₁ = 100 N × 1 m = 100 N·m, aur F₂ d₂ = 10000 N × 0.01 m = 100 N·m: barabar. Machine sirf force ko **badal** deti hai, energy paida nahi karti.\n\n**Galti 2: Radius ki jagah diameter use karna (ya ek mein radius, ek mein diameter)**\n\nArea = π r² mein **radius** aata hai. Agar piston ka diameter 4 cm diya ho to radius 2 cm hai: a = 3.14 × 2² = **12.56 cm²**. Agar 4 ko hi radius maan liya to 3.14 × 4² = 50.24 cm² aayega, jo **4 guna** galat hai. Aur ratio mein agar ek piston ka radius aur doosre ka diameter le liya, to jawab bilkul galat hoga.\n\n**Galti 3: Area ka ratio radius ke ratio jaisa le lena**\n\nRadius 1 cm aur 10 cm ho to area ka ratio **100** hai, 10 nahi (Example 5). Ratio mein **square** karna na bhoolo.\n\n**Galti 4: cm² ko m² mein badle bina Pa nikalna**\n\nP = F ÷ A mein Pa tabhi aata hai jab A m² mein ho. 1 cm² = 0.0001 m². Example 7 mein 20 cm² = 0.002 m², tab P = 100000 Pa. Agar 200 ÷ 20 = 10 ko hi Pa likh diya to galat. (Ratio A ÷ a nikalte waqt dono cm² mein ho to koi dikkat nahi.)\n\n**Galti 5: \"Bade piston par pressure zyada hota hai.\"**\n\nNahi. **Pressure dono par barabar** hai (Pascal's law). **Force** bade piston par zyada hai kyunki uska **area** zyada hai. Example 9 mein dono jagah 100000 Pa tha.\n\n**Galti 6: Pascal's law ko khule (open) system ya hawa bhare / leak wale system par laga dena**\n\nPascal's law **band** fluid ke liye hai. Agar pipe mein leak hai to tel bahar nikal jayega aur dabav ghat jayega. Agar hawa ka bubble hai to wo compress hoga (hawa compressible hai) aur force poora aage nahi pahunchega. Isiliye brake ya lift mein bubble ya leak khatarnak hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Hydraulic lift se extra energy ya work milta hai.\"**\n\nNahi. Force 100 guna hota hai to distance 100 guna ghat jata hai. Example 1 mein F₁ d₁ = 100 N × 1 m = 100 N·m, aur F₂ d₂ = 10000 N × 0.01 m = 100 N·m: barabar. Machine sirf force ko **badal** deti hai, energy paida nahi karti.\n\n**Galti 2: Radius ki jagah diameter use karna (ya ek mein radius, ek mein diameter)**\n\nArea = π r² mein **radius** aata hai. Agar piston ka diameter 4 cm diya ho to radius 2 cm hai: a = 3.14 × 2² = **12.56 cm²**. Agar 4 ko hi radius maan liya to 3.14 × 4² = 50.24 cm² aayega, jo **4 guna** galat hai. Aur ratio mein agar ek piston ka radius aur doosre ka diameter le liya, to jawab bilkul galat hoga.\n\n**Galti 3: Area ka ratio radius ke ratio jaisa le lena**\n\nRadius 1 cm aur 10 cm ho to area ka ratio **100** hai, 10 nahi (Example 5). Ratio mein **square** karna na bhoolo.\n\n**Galti 4: cm² ko m² mein badle bina Pa nikalna**\n\nP = F ÷ A mein Pa tabhi aata hai jab A m² mein ho. 1 cm² = 0.0001 m². Example 7 mein 20 cm² = 0.002 m², tab P = 100000 Pa. Agar 200 ÷ 20 = 10 ko hi Pa likh diya to galat. (Ratio A ÷ a nikalte waqt dono cm² mein ho to koi dikkat nahi.)\n\n**Galti 5: \"Bade piston par pressure zyada hota hai.\"**\n\nNahi. **Pressure dono par barabar** hai (Pascal's law). **Force** bade piston par zyada hai kyunki uska **area** zyada hai. Example 9 mein dono jagah 100000 Pa tha.\n\n**Galti 6: Pascal's law ko khule (open) system ya hawa bhare / leak wale system par laga dena**\n\nPascal's law **band** fluid ke liye hai. Agar pipe mein leak hai to tel bahar nikal jayega aur dabav ghat jayega. Agar hawa ka bubble hai to wo compress hoga (hawa compressible hai) aur force poora aage nahi pahunchega. Isiliye brake ya lift mein bubble ya leak khatarnak hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Pascal's law:** band fluid par lagaya gaya pressure fluid ke har hisse aur bartan ki deewaron tak **barabar aur bina ghate** pahunchta hai.\n- Chhed wali bottle, toothpaste tube aur chhed wala ghubbara isi ke demonstration hain.\n- **Liquid lagbhag incompressible** hai, isliye hydraulic machines mein use hota hai. **Gas compressible** hai, isliye nahi.\n- **Hydraulic lift:** F₁ ÷ a = F₂ ÷ A, yani **F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a)**. Chhote force se bada force milta hai: force multiplier.\n- Pressure dono pistons par **barabar**, force alag.\n- Distance ulta chhota: **a × d₁ = A × d₂**. **F₁ d₁ = F₂ d₂**, koi extra energy nahi (Work chapter mein aur).\n- Area ka ratio radius ke **square** ka ratio hai. Pa ke liye area **m²** mein lo (1 cm² = 0.0001 m²).\n- **Upyog:** hydraulic lift, brakes, jack, dentist ki chair, hydraulic press.\n- **Kahani ka jawab:** F₂ = 10000 N, A ÷ a = 100, F₁ = **100 N**; chhota piston 100 cm chalta hai to car sirf 1 cm uthti hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Pascal's law:** band fluid par lagaya gaya pressure fluid ke har hisse aur bartan ki deewaron tak **barabar aur bina ghate** pahunchta hai.\n- Chhed wali bottle, toothpaste tube aur chhed wala ghubbara isi ke demonstration hain.\n- **Liquid lagbhag incompressible** hai, isliye hydraulic machines mein use hota hai. **Gas compressible** hai, isliye nahi.\n- **Hydraulic lift:** F₁ ÷ a = F₂ ÷ A, yani **F₂ = F₁ × (A ÷ a)**. Chhote force se bada force milta hai: force multiplier.\n- Pressure dono pistons par **barabar**, force alag.\n- Distance ulta chhota: **a × d₁ = A × d₂**. **F₁ d₁ = F₂ d₂**, koi extra energy nahi (Work chapter mein aur).\n- Area ka ratio radius ke **square** ka ratio hai. Pa ke liye area **m²** mein lo (1 cm² = 0.0001 m²).\n- **Upyog:** hydraulic lift, brakes, jack, dentist ki chair, hydraulic press.\n- **Kahani ka jawab:** F₂ = 10000 N, A ÷ a = 100, F₁ = **100 N**; chhota piston 100 cm chalta hai to car sirf 1 cm uthti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. In a hydraulic lift the small piston has an area of 4 cm² and the large piston has an area of 400 cm². A force of 25 N is applied on the small piston. Find the force on the large piston.\n\n2. A load of 6000 N is to be lifted by a hydraulic jack. The small piston has an area of 10 cm². What force must be applied on the small piston if the large piston has an area of 1000 cm²?\n\n3. The radii of the two pistons of a hydraulic press are 2 cm and 6 cm. A force of 50 N acts on the small piston. Find the ratio of the areas of the pistons and the force on the large piston.\n\n4. A force of 100 N acts on a small piston of area 5 cm². (a) Find the pressure in the liquid in pascals. (b) The large piston has an area of 250 cm². Find the force on it.\n\n5. The small piston of a hydraulic machine (area 2 cm²) is pushed down by 40 cm. The large piston has an area of 80 cm². How far does the large piston rise? If the force on the small piston is 10 N, check that F × d is the same for both pistons.\n\n6. In a hydraulic brake, the pedal pushes the master piston of area 3 cm² with a force of 90 N. Find the pressure in the brake oil. What force acts on a wheel piston of area 12 cm²?\n\n7. State True or False:\n   (a) A hydraulic lift gives more work than the work done on the small piston.\n   (b) In a hydraulic lift the pressure on the large piston is greater than on the small piston.\n   (c) Liquids are used in hydraulic machines because they are almost incompressible.\n   (d) 1 Pa = 1 N/m².\n\n8. Why does a hydraulic brake feel \"spongy\" when an air bubble gets into the brake oil?\n\n9. A garage lift raises a car of mass 1500 kg (take g = 10 m/s²). The large piston has an area 100 times that of the small piston. What force must be applied on the small piston?\n\n10. A student says: \"If the large piston has 50 times the area, then by pushing the small piston a little I can make the car rise a lot.\" Is the student right? Explain with the distances moved by the two pistons.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. A ÷ a = 400 cm² ÷ 4 cm² = 100. F₂ = 25 N × 100 = **2500 N**.\n   Jaanch: 2500 N ÷ 100 = 25 N ✅\n\n2. A ÷ a = 1000 cm² ÷ 10 cm² = 100. F₁ = 6000 N ÷ 100 = **60 N**.\n   Jaanch: 60 N × 100 = 6000 N ✅\n\n3. Area ratio = (6 cm)² ÷ (2 cm)² = 36 ÷ 4 = **9** (radius ratio 3 hai, par area ratio 9). F₂ = 50 N × 9 = **450 N**.\n   Jaanch: 450 N ÷ 9 = 50 N ✅\n\n4. (a) a = 5 cm² = 0.0005 m². P = 100 N ÷ 0.0005 m² = **200000 Pa** (2 × 10⁵ Pa).\n   (b) A = 250 cm² = 0.025 m². F = P × A = 200000 Pa × 0.025 m² = **5000 N**.\n   Jaanch: area ratio 250 ÷ 5 = 50, aur 100 N × 50 = 5000 N ✅\n\n5. a × d₁ = A × d₂ ⇒ d₂ = (2 cm² × 40 cm) ÷ 80 cm² = 80 cm³ ÷ 80 cm² = **1 cm**. Area ratio = 80 ÷ 2 = 40, to F₂ = 10 N × 40 = 400 N.\n   F₁ d₁ = 10 N × 0.4 m = 4 N·m. F₂ d₂ = 400 N × 0.01 m = 4 N·m ✅ Barabar hain.\n\n6. a = 3 cm² = 0.0003 m². P = 90 N ÷ 0.0003 m² = **300000 Pa**. Wheel piston: A = 12 cm² = 0.0012 m². F = 300000 Pa × 0.0012 m² = **360 N**.\n   Jaanch: area ratio 12 ÷ 3 = 4, 90 N × 4 = 360 N ✅\n\n7. (a) **False** ❌. Work kabhi zyada nahi milta: force badhta hai to distance ghat jata hai (F₁ d₁ = F₂ d₂).\n   (b) **False** ❌. Pressure dono par barabar hai; bade piston par **force** zyada hai.\n   (c) **True** ✅. Incompressible hone se dabav seedha aage pahunchta hai.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Pascal ki definition yehi hai: ek newton per metre square.\n\n8. Hawa compressible hoti hai. Pedal dabane par bubble sikud jata hai, to pedal ka kaafi dabav bubble ko dabane mein hi kharch ho jata hai aur poora pressure pahiye ke piston tak nahi pahunchta. Isliye pedal neeche tak dabta hai par brake kamzor lagta hai (spongy).\n\n9. F₂ = m g = 1500 kg × 10 m/s² = 15000 N. F₁ = 15000 N ÷ 100 = **150 N**.\n   Jaanch: 150 N × 100 = 15000 N ✅\n\n10. **Aadha sahi.** Force to 50 guna ho jata hai, par \"thoda dabane se bahut upar\" galat hai. a × d₁ = A × d₂ se d₂ = d₁ ÷ 50: bada piston, chhote piston ke muqabley sirf **1/50** distance upar uthta hai. Maan lo chhote piston ko 50 cm dabaya, to bada piston sirf 1 cm uthega. Force 50 guna hai par distance 50 guna kam, to energy/work barabar rehta hai.\n    Jaanch: 50 × 1 = 1 × 50 = 50 (force × distance dono taraf barabar) ✅\n\n---\n\nAb aap Tanmay ko bata sakte ho ki ek tonne ki car ko sirf 100 N ke push se kaise uthaya jata hai: **pressure barabar, area bada, to force bada**, aur kaam ka hisaab distance se barabar. Agli baar kisi service station par lift dekho to usme chhupa ye niyam pehchaan lena.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab tak hum liquid ke andar dabav dekhte rahe. Ab liquid se **hawa ki taraf**: **hawa ka bhi bojh hota hai.** Hum sab ek aise samundar ke neeche rehte hain jo dikhta nahi, aur uska pressure bhi hum par lagta hai. Ye hum **Topic 4** mein dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. In a hydraulic lift the small piston has an area of 4 cm² and the large piston has an area of 400 cm². A force of 25 N is applied on the small piston. Find the force on the large piston.\n\n2. A load of 6000 N is to be lifted by a hydraulic jack. The small piston has an area of 10 cm². What force must be applied on the small piston if the large piston has an area of 1000 cm²?\n\n3. The radii of the two pistons of a hydraulic press are 2 cm and 6 cm. A force of 50 N acts on the small piston. Find the ratio of the areas of the pistons and the force on the large piston.\n\n4. A force of 100 N acts on a small piston of area 5 cm². (a) Find the pressure in the liquid in pascals. (b) The large piston has an area of 250 cm². Find the force on it.\n\n5. The small piston of a hydraulic machine (area 2 cm²) is pushed down by 40 cm. The large piston has an area of 80 cm². How far does the large piston rise? If the force on the small piston is 10 N, check that F × d is the same for both pistons.\n\n6. In a hydraulic brake, the pedal pushes the master piston of area 3 cm² with a force of 90 N. Find the pressure in the brake oil. What force acts on a wheel piston of area 12 cm²?\n\n7. State True or False:\n   (a) A hydraulic lift gives more work than the work done on the small piston.\n   (b) In a hydraulic lift the pressure on the large piston is greater than on the small piston.\n   (c) Liquids are used in hydraulic machines because they are almost incompressible.\n   (d) 1 Pa = 1 N/m².\n\n8. Why does a hydraulic brake feel \"spongy\" when an air bubble gets into the brake oil?\n\n9. A garage lift raises a car of mass 1500 kg (take g = 10 m/s²). The large piston has an area 100 times that of the small piston. What force must be applied on the small piston?\n\n10. A student says: \"If the large piston has 50 times the area, then by pushing the small piston a little I can make the car rise a lot.\" Is the student right? Explain with the distances moved by the two pistons.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. A ÷ a = 400 cm² ÷ 4 cm² = 100. F₂ = 25 N × 100 = **2500 N**.\n   Jaanch: 2500 N ÷ 100 = 25 N ✅\n\n2. A ÷ a = 1000 cm² ÷ 10 cm² = 100. F₁ = 6000 N ÷ 100 = **60 N**.\n   Jaanch: 60 N × 100 = 6000 N ✅\n\n3. Area ratio = (6 cm)² ÷ (2 cm)² = 36 ÷ 4 = **9** (radius ratio 3 hai, par area ratio 9). F₂ = 50 N × 9 = **450 N**.\n   Jaanch: 450 N ÷ 9 = 50 N ✅\n\n4. (a) a = 5 cm² = 0.0005 m². P = 100 N ÷ 0.0005 m² = **200000 Pa** (2 × 10⁵ Pa).\n   (b) A = 250 cm² = 0.025 m². F = P × A = 200000 Pa × 0.025 m² = **5000 N**.\n   Jaanch: area ratio 250 ÷ 5 = 50, aur 100 N × 50 = 5000 N ✅\n\n5. a × d₁ = A × d₂ ⇒ d₂ = (2 cm² × 40 cm) ÷ 80 cm² = 80 cm³ ÷ 80 cm² = **1 cm**. Area ratio = 80 ÷ 2 = 40, to F₂ = 10 N × 40 = 400 N.\n   F₁ d₁ = 10 N × 0.4 m = 4 N·m. F₂ d₂ = 400 N × 0.01 m = 4 N·m ✅ Barabar hain.\n\n6. a = 3 cm² = 0.0003 m². P = 90 N ÷ 0.0003 m² = **300000 Pa**. Wheel piston: A = 12 cm² = 0.0012 m². F = 300000 Pa × 0.0012 m² = **360 N**.\n   Jaanch: area ratio 12 ÷ 3 = 4, 90 N × 4 = 360 N ✅\n\n7. (a) **False** ❌. Work kabhi zyada nahi milta: force badhta hai to distance ghat jata hai (F₁ d₁ = F₂ d₂).\n   (b) **False** ❌. Pressure dono par barabar hai; bade piston par **force** zyada hai.\n   (c) **True** ✅. Incompressible hone se dabav seedha aage pahunchta hai.\n   (d) **True** ✅. Pascal ki definition yehi hai: ek newton per metre square.\n\n8. Hawa compressible hoti hai. Pedal dabane par bubble sikud jata hai, to pedal ka kaafi dabav bubble ko dabane mein hi kharch ho jata hai aur poora pressure pahiye ke piston tak nahi pahunchta. Isliye pedal neeche tak dabta hai par brake kamzor lagta hai (spongy).\n\n9. F₂ = m g = 1500 kg × 10 m/s² = 15000 N. F₁ = 15000 N ÷ 100 = **150 N**.\n   Jaanch: 150 N × 100 = 15000 N ✅\n\n10. **Aadha sahi.** Force to 50 guna ho jata hai, par \"thoda dabane se bahut upar\" galat hai. a × d₁ = A × d₂ se d₂ = d₁ ÷ 50: bada piston, chhote piston ke muqabley sirf **1/50** distance upar uthta hai. Maan lo chhote piston ko 50 cm dabaya, to bada piston sirf 1 cm uthega. Force 50 guna hai par distance 50 guna kam, to energy/work barabar rehta hai.\n    Jaanch: 50 × 1 = 1 × 50 = 50 (force × distance dono taraf barabar) ✅\n\n---\n\nAb aap Tanmay ko bata sakte ho ki ek tonne ki car ko sirf 100 N ke push se kaise uthaya jata hai: **pressure barabar, area bada, to force bada**, aur kaam ka hisaab distance se barabar. Agli baar kisi service station par lift dekho to usme chhupa ye niyam pehchaan lena.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab tak hum liquid ke andar dabav dekhte rahe. Ab liquid se **hawa ki taraf**: **hawa ka bhi bojh hota hai.** Hum sab ek aise samundar ke neeche rehte hain jo dikhta nahi, aur uska pressure bhi hum par lagta hai. Ye hum **Topic 4** mein dekhenge."
          }
